00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:06.869 align:start position:0%
european union home affairs ministers  have examined ways to prevent  tragedies in the mediterranean sea as  would-be immigrants head for europe's  shores  in poorly maintained boats the ministers  looked at what steps they can take in  the short term  to help avoid tragedies like the one off  the island of lampedusa in october  the debate was based on priority actions  identified by the task force for the  mediterranean  these include increased cooperation with  third countries  better protection and resettlement  measures boosting the fight against  trafficking and smuggling  tougher border surveillance and more  solidarity with member states under high  migration pressure  the tragedies at sea will be a focus  when european leaders meet in brussels  in june  ministers also had in-depth talks on the  issue of fighters from europe crossing  into syria  to join the conflict there most of them  alongside jihadist groups  they looked at ways to better prevent  the recruitment of europeans  improve information exchange beef up the  criminal justice response and boost  cooperation with  other countries concerned the flow of  foreign fighters traveling to syria from  the eu and other countries has been  gradually rising  and some could pose a security threat  when they return home  for justice ministers data protection  was a priority concern  they discussed new ways to better  protect personal information  notably the so-called one-stop shop  principle  it would see one national authority  monitor businesses or organizations that  process  personal data in more than one country  ideally the authority in the state where  the business operates  the ministers underlined the need to  ensure that quality reforms are put in  place  which would improve the internal market  and the competitiveness of european  companies  without sacrificing privacy
 

